X:1
T:Open Now Thy Gates of Beauty
T:UNSER HERRSCHER
C:Music: Joachim Neander, 1680 · Words: Benjamin Schmolck, 1730. Translated by Catherine Winkworth, 1863
S:Open Hymnal Project ABC (openhymnal.org; marked public domain), melody line with every stanza as printed there; their source: "Chorale Book for England", 1863 Hymn 15.. No second score was available to cross-check the melody.
M:4/4
L:1/4
Q:1/4=100
K:Bb
B,3/2 C/ D B, | D E F F | B A/G/ F d | c c B2 |
w: Op- en now thy gates of beau- ty, Zi- on, * let me en- ter there,
w: Lord, my God, I come be- fore Thee, Come Thou * al- so un- to me;
w: Here Thy praise is glad- ly chant- ed, Here Thy * seed is du- ly sown;
w: Thou my faith in- crease and quick- en, Let me * keep Thy gift di- vine,
w: Speak, O God, and I will hear Thee, Let Thy * will be done in- deed;
B,3/2 C/ D B, | D E F F | B A/G/ F d | c c B2 |
w: Where my soul in joy- ful du- ty Waits for * Him Who an- swers prayer.
w: Where we find Thee and a- dore Thee, There a * heav'n on earth must be.
w: Let my soul, where it is plant- ed, Bring forth * pre- cious sheaves a- lone,
w: How- so- e'er temp- ta- tions thick- en; May Thy * Word still o'er me shine
w: May I un- dist- urbed draw near Thee While Thou * dost Thy peo- ple feed.
d3/2 d/ c c | B B A2 | G3/2 G/ F B | c A B2 |]
w: Oh, how bless- èd is this place, Filled with so- lace, light and grace!
w: To my heart, oh, en- ter Thou, Let it be Thy tem- ple now!
w: So that all I hear may be Fruit- ful un- to life in me.
w: As my guid- ing star through life, As my com- fort in my strife.
w: Here of life the foun- tain flows, Here is balm for all our woes.
